# Negative control — the retry verb falls silent instead of refusing, so a
# client who reaches it by a kept link is told nothing at all: no refusal, no
# toast, no answer. A control the client pressed always answers (plan R4's
# second tier) — silence is what an UNKNOWN verb earns, never a known one.
#
# RETARGETED 2026-09-06: this graded `retry()`'s own already-arrived guard,
# and that write was deleted with the over-build (sending a client's message
# again is the desk's job). The behaviour it protected now lives in the
# dispatcher's refusal, so the mutation moves there — and mutates the ANSWER
# rather than the write, which `f11-email-verb-succeeds` already grades. The
# two titles it used to name went with the writes; the one below folded them.
#
# Intended RED: "refuses either message, whatever its standing, and leaves it
# where it stands" (tests/email-history-actions.test.ts); "refuses both verbs
# as ours to run, and writes nothing, on either brand"
# (tests/seventh-closure.test.ts).

